= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the specified self-dual-pairing meaning of “coseparable”; categorically this is the <Frobenius monoid> structure relevant here. The induced <monoid object> on $FA$ has maps
$$
\boxed{J=F(j)\varphi_0,\qquad M=F(m)\varphi_{A,A},\qquad E_A=\psi_0F(\varepsilon).}
$$
Its associativity follows from associativity of $m$ and the <lax monoidal functor> axiom for $\varphi$: both iterated products are $F$ of the corresponding triple product, preceded by the same coherent iterated tensor comparison. The unit laws follow from those of $j,m$ and the two monoidal unit axioms. Thus $(FA,J,M)$ is a <monoid object>.

Its pairing is exactly the evaluation transported in the preceding part:
$$
E_AM=\psi_0F(\varepsilon)F(m)\varphi_{A,A}=\psi_0F(\varepsilon m)\varphi_{A,A}.
$$
If $n:I\to A\otimes A$ is the original <coevaluation morphism>, the corresponding new one is $\psi_{A,A}F(n)\varphi_0$. The preceding <Frobenius monoidal functor> calculation proves both <snake identities>. Hence **the pairing of the induced monoid remains self-dual**, giving the claimed canonical structure on $FA$. The terminology in the paper imposes this duality condition; it does not add a separability or semisimplicity condition.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**The algebra must be finite-dimensional, and there is no universal bound on its dimension.** Let $e=\varepsilon m$ and write its <coevaluation morphism> as a finite sum
$$
n(1)=\sum_{i=1}^r a_i\otimes b_i.
$$
For any $v\in A$, one <snake identity> gives
$$
v=\sum_{i=1}^r e(v,a_i)b_i.
$$
Thus the finitely many $b_i$ span $A$, making it a <finite-dimensional vector space>. The other <snake identity> shows that $e$ is <nondegenerate> in the other variable as well, so the maps to the <dual space> determined by this pairing are isomorphisms. In the standard algebraic terminology this is a <Frobenius algebra>.

Every positive finite <dimension of a vector space> occurs: take $A=k^d$ with coordinatewise multiplication and $\varepsilon(a_1,\ldots,a_d)=\sum_i a_i$. The coordinate idempotents $e_i$ give $e(e_i,e_j)=\delta_{ij}$ and $n(1)=\sum_i e_i\otimes e_i$. If zero unital algebras are allowed, the zero object gives dimension zero too.

The pairing need not make $A$ a <semisimple algebra>. For example, the <dual numbers> $k[t]/(t^2)$ with $\varepsilon(a+bt)=b$ have pairing matrix
$$
\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}
$$
and <coevaluation morphism> $1\mapsto1\otimes t+t\otimes1$. This pairing is <nondegenerate> over every <field>, despite the <nilpotent ideal> $(t)$. **Finite-dimensionality is the dimension conclusion; separability is not part of the given definition.**

